.TH FUNC 1
.SH NAME
func \- name the function each place is in
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B func
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Func
copies its standard input to its standard output, and in each line that names a place,
.IB file : line : col :
or
.IB file : line :\f1,
puts the function the place is in after the place, as
.B git grep -p
does: the nearest line above the place that the function pattern matches.
The pattern is the
.B func=
of the first rule in
.B $home/lib/lsp
or
.B /lib/lsp
(see
.IR lspfs (4))
whose
.B file=
matches the file.
If the pattern has a group, the function's name is what the group matches;
otherwise it is the whole line, without its indent or a trailing
.LR { .
A file no rule names takes git's pattern: a line that starts with a letter,
.L _
or
.LR $ .
Lines that are not places, and places with no function above them, are copied as they are.
.SH EXAMPLES
Plan 9's C puts a function's name at the start of a line, so a rule for it can say
.IP
.EX
lsp=clangd cmd=clangd file="\e.[ch]$" func="^([a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*)\e("
.EE
.PP
and then
.IP
.EX
grep -n textinsert /sys/src/cmd/acme/*.c | func
.EE
.PP
gives lines such as
.IP
.EX
/sys/src/cmd/acme/text.c:420: textinsert: textinsert(t, q0, r, n, tofile);
.EE
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/func.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR grep (1),
.IR alsp (4),
.IR lspfs (4)
